`const data: VehicleContent = await response.json()` is a **type assertion in disguise**: `json()` returns `any`, so the compiler trusts you. If the API returns `heroImage: null`, a renamed field or an error body with status 200, TypeScript will not notice and the crash happens later.

## Validate at the boundary

Parse external data once, where it enters your system, and let the rest of the code rely on the validated type. A sketch using Zod (a popular library; check the API of the version you use):

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

export const VehicleContentSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  title: z.string().min(1),
  description: z.string(),
  heroImage: z.object({
    url: z.string(),
    altText: z.string(),
  }),
});

export type VehicleContent = z.infer<typeof VehicleContentSchema>;

export async function fetchVehicle(slug: string, signal?: AbortSignal) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/content/vehicles/${encodeURIComponent(slug)}`, { signal });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Content request failed: ${res.status}`);
  return VehicleContentSchema.parse(await res.json()); // throws ZodError if the shape is wrong
}
```

## Design decisions

- **Strict or tolerant?** Be strict on fields you depend on, but ignore unknown extra fields so additive API changes do not break you.
- **Fail loudly in monitoring, softly for users.** Log the validation issue with the content ID, then render a fallback or last-known-good content.
- **Single source of truth.** Infer the type from the schema, so they cannot diverge.
- **Cost.** Validation has a small runtime cost; for very large payloads validate only what you use, or validate on the server (BFF or SSR) rather than in every browser.

## Complementary tools

Runtime validation catches drift at the consumer; **contract tests** and generated types catch it earlier, in CI. Use both.
